From: arch-m4 project
Subject: [PATCH] nvme: remove the failed Wallace forced ANS cold reset

Reverse the exact t6040-nvme-apple-force-clean-ans-boot.patch from Wallace
55f116dd0e0f28d1412c6be728a101f7d861c473 (patch SHA-256
399578f56d4ec6ae1d865be68878b68dad6868d1a56015b085cd088592994904).
The original workaround failed on the J614s (Wallace ticket 192). The
new loader explicitly initializes and shuts down ANS before Linux. Keep
upstream reset/wake logic rather than forcing a running coprocessor off.

--- a/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
@@ -1098,19 +1098,6 @@
 	}
 
 	/*
-	 * A running coprocessor that this RTKit instance never booted was
-	 * left live by an earlier boot stage (e.g. iBoot through a loader
-	 * without NVMe support). Its endpoint/shared-memory state is not
-	 * ours to wake into; force a stop so the clean boot path below runs.
-	 */
-	if (!apple_rtkit_is_running(anv->rtk) &&
-	    (readl(anv->mmio_coproc + APPLE_ANS_COPROC_CPU_CONTROL) &
-	     APPLE_ANS_COPROC_CPU_CONTROL_RUN)) {
-		dev_warn(anv->dev, "ANS left running by boot stage; forcing clean reset\n");
-		writel(0, anv->mmio_coproc + APPLE_ANS_COPROC_CPU_CONTROL);
-	}
-
-	/*
 	 * Only do the soft-reset if the CPU is not running, which means either we
 	 * or the previous stage shut it down cleanly.
 	 */
